] Hu et al. — LoRA (ICLR 2022)\n[10] Ranzato et al. — Sequence level training (ICLR 2016)\n[11] Bengio et al. — Scheduled sampling (NeurIPS 2015)\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the contributions? Is \"gating doesn't work for multi-turn attention\" a surprising or expected finding? Does the mechanistic analysis (gradient conflict, distribution shift) provide genuine new insight?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key related works missing? Is the positioning accurate? Are there important baselines or methods from the attention steering / exposure bias / gating literature that should be discussed?\n3. **Scope of claims vs evidence**: Do the authors overclaim or underclaim? Is the generalization from \"one specific gating approach fails\" to \"all training-time attention steering faces fundamental barriers\" warranted?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Would the ML community benefit from this negative result? Does it redirect research effort effectively?\n5. **Writing quality and framing**: Is the narrative clear? Is the negative-result framing appropriate?\n\nConsider these specific questions:\n- Only 11 references for an analysis paper — is this sufficient?\n- The paper claims implications for \"all auxiliary attention losses\" — is this supported by testing only one?\n- Is the distribution shift insight genuinely new, or is it a known consequence of exposure bias applied to a new setting?\n- Missing related work: attention sink [Xiao et al. 2024], StreamingLLM, multi-turn specific methods, prompt compression, context distillation, KV cache management, etc.?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, with severity: Minor/Moderate/Major)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Related Work** (specific papers/areas)\n- **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n- **Confidence** (1-5 scale)