{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FqCxTK1nnbYaJCPuemUy1F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kf3CruQnrZ7xe24MpveDbz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/turn_residual_gating/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135115/best/paper.tex","offset":0,"limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11071,"cache_read_input_tokens":59260,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11071,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011dmdW3GZyHJXDsjZrxj7EN","session_id":"a8db08cb-5504-474b-a8d9-cc4664a111cc","uuid":"2d7efef6-76d2-4fd9-9c36-1d09d8542543"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kf3CruQnrZ7xe24MpveDbz","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n2\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n4\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{array}\n9\t\\usepackage{float}\n10\t\n11\t\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue}\n12\t\n13\t\\newcommand{\\gar}{\\textsc{GAR}}\n14\t\\newcommand{\\tg}{\\textsc{ThreadGate}}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\ug}{\\textsc{UniformGate}}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Why Lightweight Gating Cannot Fix Multi-Turn Attention Degradation:\\\\A Mechanistic Analysis}\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t% ============================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t% ============================================================\n27\tMulti-turn dialogue performance in large language models degrades as conversations grow longer.\n28\tRecent work by Dongre et al.\\ quantified this through the Goal Attention Ratio (\\gar{}), showing that attention to goal-relevant tokens drops by 27--48\\% over extended conversations, while residual stream probes reveal that goal information persists (AUC up to 0.98).\n29\tThis creates a natural hypothesis: a learnable gate on the residual connection should recover goal attention.\n30\tWe implement this hypothesis as \\tg{}, a per-layer gating module trained with joint next-token prediction (NTP) and \\gar{} objectives.\n31\t\\tg{} successfully optimizes \\gar{} during training ($0.02 \\to 0.48$), but this improvement completely fails to transfer to inference (\\gar{}$= 0.016$, a $30\\times$ gap).\n32\tWe identify three coupled failure mechanisms: (1)~NTP gradients dominate gate parameters (magnitude ratio $7.8$--$14.2\\times$), causing gates to learn \\emph{reversed} turn conditioning; (2)~the resulting $30\\times$ training--inference \\gar{} gap reflects a structural distribution shift from teacher-forcing to autoregressive generation; (3)~at inference time, gates attenuate residual updates by only 2--7\\%, far too weak to redirect attention.\n33\tFour-way ablation (\\tg{} vs.\\ Uniform Gate vs.\\ LoRA-only vs.\\ Base) on two architecture families (Qwen-2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B) confirms that gating provides zero incremental contribution.\n34\tA downstream instruction-retention task correlates strongly with \\gar{} ($r = 0.89$), validating \\gar{} as a behavioral proxy.\n35\tOur results demonstrate that auxiliary attention losses optimized under teacher-forcing face a fundamental generalization barrier, with implications for all attention steering methods.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t% ============================================================\n42\t\n43\tLarge language models increasingly serve as multi-turn conversational agents, yet their performance degrades as dialogues extend beyond the first few turns.\n44\tUsers report that models ``forget'' system-prompt instructions, lose track of conversation goals, and produce increasingly generic responses in later turns.\n45\tThis degradation has practical consequences for deployment in customer service, tutoring, and collaborative writing, where conversations routinely span tens of turns.\n46\t\n47\tDongre et al.~\\cite{dongre2026} provided the first systematic quantification of this phenomenon through the Goal Attention Ratio (\\gar{}), measuring the fraction of attention that response tokens allocate to goal-relevant tokens in the system prompt.\n48\tAcross 12 model families, they observed \\gar{} drops of 27--48\\% over 50 turns.\n49\tCrucially, their residual stream probing experiments revealed that goal information \\emph{persists} in the residual stream (linear probe AUC up to 0.98) even as the attention channel closes."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011dmdW3GZyHJXDsjZrxj7EN","session_id":"a8db08cb-5504-474b-a8d9-cc4664a111cc","uuid":"d5fdf5f9-2d82-49f4-8322-6b4bb02a7454","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:27:54.321Z"}
