{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FqCxTK1nnbYaJCPuemUy1F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PbHR1kffu3rtocA8SHmV6Y","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/turn_residual_gating/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135115/worst/paper.tex","offset":0,"limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11071,"cache_read_input_tokens":59260,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11071,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011dmdW3GZyHJXDsjZrxj7EN","session_id":"a8db08cb-5504-474b-a8d9-cc4664a111cc","uuid":"65038280-a200-430e-ad4a-0176aa166563"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PbHR1kffu3rtocA8SHmV6Y","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n2\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n9\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\n12\t\\title{Failure Mode Taxonomy of Residual Gating for Multi-Turn Attention Recovery:\\\\A Mechanistic Case Study under Next-Token Prediction Dominance}\n13\t\n14\t\\author{Anonymous}\n15\t\\date{}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t% =========================================================================\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tMulti-turn dialogue performance in large language models degrades as conversations lengthen, with goal attention ratio (GAR) declining by 48--71\\% across architectures. Prior work shows that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream even as the attention channel closes, motivating residual gating as an architectural intervention. We implement ThreadGate, a per-layer gating module trained jointly with next-token prediction (NTP) and GAR objectives, and conduct a systematic study spanning seven loss configurations (v1--v7) and four-way ablation (ThreadGate / Uniform Gate / LoRA-only / Base). On Qwen-2.5-7B, ThreadGate achieves 0.48 training GAR but only 0.032 at inference on held-out data, revealing a 15$\\times$ training-inference gap that persists across evaluation sets. Four-way ablation shows ThreadGate provides only marginal, non-significant improvement over LoRA-only ($\\Delta$=0.010, $p$=0.08). Cross-architecture analysis on Llama-3.1-8B reveals architecture-dependent susceptibility: GAR degradation is milder (48\\% vs.\\ 71\\%), the training-inference gap shrinks to 2.4$\\times$, and gate reversal affects only 34\\% of layers versus 96\\% in Qwen. NTP gradient magnitude dominates GAR gradients by 3--6$\\times$, though directional conflict is noisy and architecture-dependent. Downstream instruction retention correlates weakly with GAR ($r$=0.31, $p$=0.18), suggesting GAR captures only part of the multi-turn degradation phenomenon. We contribute a failure mode taxonomy---reverse turn conditioning, training-inference distribution shift, and insufficient gate intervention---and discuss implications for auxiliary attention losses optimized under teacher forcing.\n23\t\\end{abstract}\n24\t\n25\t% =========================================================================\n26\t\\section{Introduction}\n27\t\n28\tLarge language models exhibit systematic performance degradation in multi-turn dialogue as conversation length increases \\citep{dongre2026gar}. This degradation manifests across multiple dimensions: instruction following accuracy drops, goal-relevant information receives progressively less attention, and response quality declines. Dongre et al.\\ quantify this through the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), documenting 27--48\\% drops over extended conversations across twelve models, while their residual stream probes reveal that goal information persists (AUC up to 0.98) even as the attention channel closes.\n29\t\n30\tThis creates a natural hypothesis: if goal information remains in the residual stream but attention fails to access it, a learnable gate on the residual connection should recover it. We test this hypothesis through ThreadGate, a per-layer gating module that modulates residual stream updates based on learned attention steering signals, trained jointly with NTP and GAR objectives.\n31\t\n32\tOur investigation yields a detailed taxonomy of failure modes rather than a clean negative result. On Qwen-2.5-7B, ThreadGate successfully optimizes GAR during training (0.02$\\to$0.48), but on held-out conversations the training-inference gap is 15$\\times$, with inference GAR reaching only 0.032. Four-way ablation (ThreadGate vs.\\ Uniform Gate vs.\\ LoRA-only vs.\\ Base) reveals that gating provides marginal, statistically non-significant improvement over LoRA adaptation alone. We identify three coupled failure mechanisms: (1) \\textbf{reverse turn conditioning}---NTP gradients push gates toward identity, causing 96\\% of layers to suppress intervention where it is most needed; (2) \\textbf{training-inference distribution shift}---attention patterns learned under teacher forcing do not transfer to autoregressive generation; (3) \\textbf{insufficient intervention magnitude}---gates attenuate residual updates by only 2--7\\% at inference.\n33\t\n34\tCross-architecture analysis on Llama-3.1-8B reveals that both the severity of GAR degradation and the effectiveness of gating are architecture-dependent. Llama-3 exhibits milder GAR decay (48\\% vs.\\ 71\\%), a much smaller training-inference gap (2.4$\\times$), and gate reversal in only 34\\% of layers. These differences challenge any universality claim but reinforce the value of architecture-specific mechanistic analysis.\n35\t\n36\tOur contributions are: (1) a detailed failure mode taxonomy of residual gating under NTP dominance, grounded in a systematic design space exploration (v1--v7) and four-way ablation; (2) quantitative evidence that teacher-forcing distribution shift degrades auxiliary attention loss generalization, with gap magnitudes of 2.4--15$\\times$ depending on architecture; (3) cross-architecture comparison revealing architecture-dependent susceptibility to multi-turn attention degradation; (4) a downstream sanity check establishing the partial but imperfect correspondence between GAR and instruction retention.\n37\t\n38\t% =========================================================================\n39\t\\section{Related Work}\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn dialogue degradation.}\n42\tPerformance degradation in extended conversations has been documented across model families \\citep{dongre2026gar}. Attention dilution, where goal-relevant tokens receive progressively less attention as context grows, is one proposed mechanism. Dongre et al.\\ introduce GAR as a quantitative measure and show that goal information persists in residual streams despite attention channel closure.\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Residual gating and adaptive computation.}\n45\tGating mechanisms on residual connections have been explored for adaptive depth \\citep{elbayad2020depth}, mixture of experts \\citep{shazeer2017moe}, and layer-selective computation. Our work applies per-layer scalar gating specifically to steer attention toward goal tokens, differing from prior work in objective (attention recovery rather than efficiency).\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Attention steering and intervention.}\n48\tMethods for steering attention include attention supervision \\citep{liu2016attn}, auxiliary attention losses, and inference-time intervention \\citep{li2023iti}. Our work connects to the exposure bias literature \\citep{ranzato2016sequence, bengio2015scheduled}: teacher-forcing creates distributional mismatch between training and inference, which we show specifically disrupts auxiliary attention loss generalization.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011dmdW3GZyHJXDsjZrxj7EN","session_id":"a8db08cb-5504-474b-a8d9-cc4664a111cc","uuid":"d2c3d02d-9f6e-41f7-b6d9-0c78efb3d7e1","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:27:55.155Z"}
